taking $\pi_{K'}$ to be the trivial group (that is $K'=k$), one would find a corresponding homomorphism $K\longrightarrow k$!

One must at the very least, in order to work at ease starting from homomorphisms $\pi_{K'}-\pi_K$ (instead of $E_{K'}-E_K$) assume that the centralizer in $\pi_K$ of the image of every open subgroup of $\pi_{K'}$ is reduced to $(1)$ --- we shall say that the homomorphism in question is \uline{anabelian} let us say --- in such a way that from this homomorphism (commuting with $\Gamma$) one reconstructs the homomorphism of extensions $E_K$ and $E_{K'}$, which is the really essential object. For example if precisely $K'=k$, hence $E_{K'}=\Gamma$, that \unsure{which|which} interests us, it is not the $\Gamma$-homomorphisms from $\pi_{K'}=(1)$ (!) into $\pi_K$, but rather the \uline{sections} of $E_K$ over $\Gamma$.

\uline{Question-conjecture} Let $K,K'$ be two fields, \unsure{finite|finite}\inserted{-type} extensions of $\mathbb Q$, \deleted{let us consider} \inserted{and} a morphism $B_{K'}\longrightarrow B_K$ of topoi over $B_{\mathbb Q}$.
